# Instructions

At this proposed NEW paragraph boundary, has the preceding spoken turn ended by trailing off or interruption, with the target beginning outside that turn?

Locate the nearest genuine main-reading predecessor in the supplied raw local evidence. A closing quote after an ellipsis is only a candidate cue. Do not invent missing words, speakers, indentation or a predecessor outside the supplied neighborhood.

A deliberately unfinished utterance can finish a spoken turn: later independent narration about an interruption or a new utterance need not grammatically complete that abandoned utterance. Do not require the words inside a deliberately ended spoken turn to form a complete sentence.

The outside grammatical frame must nevertheless be complete. If an outer conditional or subordinate clause containing the quoted speech still awaits its main clause or obligatory complement from the target, answer FALSE. Also reject if the target continues the same quoted utterance or merely supplies a missing obligatory component rather than begins outside it.

This proves only that this syntax break is permissible under the ended-turn convention, not a word boundary, ownership, other gaps, or OCR correctness. If the actual ended-turn-to-independent-content relationship is not established, answer FALSE.

# True

The spoken turn has ended and the target begins independently outside it; no unfinished outer frame requires this join.

# False

The independent content after an ended turn is not established, or a required outside construction still continues.
